## repo_readonly — repository

Repository root (read-only): /home/user/credit-memo-agent/.bridgie/worktrees/statement-review/T01-review
Read any file you need to judge the work in context. Do not modify anything.

## lane_attacks — lanes/engineering/attacks.md

# Engineering attack list (reviewer)
- Double submit: the same request or event arrives twice. Side effects once?
- Concurrent update: two writers race on the same row or aggregate.
- Boundary values: zero, negative, max, empty, null, unicode, very long.
- Currency and timezone: minor units, zero-decimal currencies, DST, UTC vs local.
- Partial failure: the first write succeeds and the second fails; the worker dies mid-batch.
- Authz bypass: new endpoint or handler missing the scope or permission check.
- Tenant isolation: a query without the tenant filter.
- Changed contract consumers: every consumer of a changed DTO, field or event, including web, mobile, PDF, email.
- Migration safety: reversible, additive first, backfill size.
- Error paths: exceptions swallowed, retries without backoff, logs with secrets.

## gotchas — gotchas (project, then lane)

# Gotchas

Dated one-line corrections learned in this project.
- 2026-11-18 Mark tests so the runner runs them by name: a test that proves a correctness property P<n> gets `@pytest.mark.invariants` and has `p<n>` in its name; interface and data-format tests get `@pytest.mark.contracts`; tests comparing the v1 rule table with today's results get `@pytest.mark.dual_run`. The reviewer only accepts a test result the runner produced, and unmarked tests reach it only as an aggregate count.
- 2026-11-18 In claim.json, claim only what the runner's configured tiers prove (ruff, compileall, the full pytest suite, the offline evals, the marker tiers). Leave out claims about wheel installs, historical replays or "no skips" unless a configured tier shows them; an unproven claim becomes a reviewer finding.
- 2026-11-18 Keep helper scripts out of the run folder (.bridgie/runs/...). Put test helpers under tests/ in the worktree; anything a seat writes under .bridgie/ fails the run.
- 2026-11-18 A requirement is done only when production code calls it: a building block with no caller yet (e.g. a case-log append that no command records into) is failed by the reviewer for that requirement.
- 2026-11-18 Install the project with all extras before verifying (pip install -e ".[dev,mcp,durable,llm]"); without the durable extra the SQLite checkpoint tests are skipped and resume claims cannot be proven.


# Engineering lane gotchas
- 2026-08: a shared-contract field change ran seven review rounds without the web consumer ever appearing in the packet; always trace consumers with a command, not memory.
- 2026-09: a diff packet that included the run directory grew to 3.1M characters and the reviewer refused it; packets exclude `.bridgie/runs/**`.
- 2026-08: a QA seat without repository access invented a finding with a line number that did not exist; verifiers must read the repo or abort.

# Output contract

End your reply with exactly one fenced ```json block containing your evidence.json (or write it to /home/user/credit-memo-agent/.bridgie/runs/statement-review/reviewer-13/evidence.json).
